%I A400232 #12 Sep 29 2026 06:40:49
%S A400232 8,25,48,71,88,93,80,43,-24,-127,-272,-465,-712,-1019,-1392,-1837,
%T A400232 -2360,-2967,-3664,-4457,-5352,-6355,-7472,-8709,-10072,-11567,-13200,
%U A400232 -14977,-16904,-18987,-21232,-23645,-26232,-28999,-31952,-35097,-38440,-41987,-45744,-49717
%N A400232 a(n) is the number of boundary lattice points minus the number of interior lattice points of the cube [0,n] X [0,n] X [0,n].
%C A400232 The cube has (n-1)^3 interior lattice points (those with every coordinate strictly between 0 and n) and 6*n^2 + 2 boundary lattice points. a(n) is the boundary count minus the interior count.
%C A400232 a(n) reaches a maximum of 93 at n = 6 and is negative for n >= 9, since the interior count grows like n^3 while the boundary count grows like n^2.
%C A400232 More generally, for a d-dimensional cube [0,n]^d, the same difference equals (n+1)^d - 2*(n-1)^d. The present sequence is the case d = 3. The case d = 2, the n X n square, is (essentially) a restatement of Pick's theorem. The case d = 4 is A400233. The first n at which this difference becomes negative, as a function of d, is A342831.
%H A400232 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400232/b400232.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400232 a(n) = -n^3 + 9*n^2 - 3*n + 3.
%F A400232 a(n) = 6*n^2 + 2 - (n-1)^3.
%e A400232 The cube [0,4]^3 has 125 lattice points in total, of which 27 are interior and 98 are on the boundary, so a(4) = 98 - 27 = 71.
%t A400232 A400232[n_] := n*(n*(9 - n) - 3) + 3; Array[A400232, 50] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 29 2026 *)
%o A400232 (PARI) a(n) = -n^3 + 9*n^2 - 3*n + 3
%o A400232 (Python) def a(n): return -n**3 + 9*n**2 - 3*n + 3
%Y A400232 Cf. A342831, A400233, A400235.
%K A400232 sign,easy,new
%O A400232 1,1
%A A400232 _Henrik Arhold_, Sep 21 2026
